INSERT INTO _s496_b5_c VALUES
(2939,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'matthew',25,40,$n$*Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren.*$n$,$n$Matthew 25:40 — *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) Said to the goats it is *ye did it not to me*, and said to the sheep it is *ye have done it unto me*. The same people stand behind both sentences, the least of these, and the sheep's version names them his *brethren*, so what was withheld on the left was withheld from his own kin.$n$),
(2940,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'matthew',12,46,$n$*Whosoever shall do the will of my Father ... the same is my brother.*$n$,$n$Matthew 12:46 — *While he yet talked to the people, behold, his mother and his brethren stood without, desiring to speak with him.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) When his mother and his brethren stand outside, he stretches his hand toward his disciples and says *Behold my mother and my brethren!* (Matthew 12:49). The brethren of the throne belong to that same family, those he claims by the Father's will and not a crowd of strangers.$n$),
(2941,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'matthew',12,46,$n$*Whosoever shall do the will of my Father ... the same is my brother.*$n$,$n$Matthew 12:46 — *While he yet talked to the people, behold, his mother and his brethren stood without, desiring to speak with him.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) When he is asked about his brethren he turns to his disciples and says *Behold my mother and my brethren!* (Matthew 12:49). The ones the goats passed by in hunger and prison are counted in that family, and so the neglect is counted as done to him.$n$),
(2942,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'matthew',28,10,$n$*Go tell my brethren that they go into Galilee.*$n$,$n$Matthew 28:10 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto them, Be not afraid: go tell my brethren that they go into Galilee, and there shall they see me.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) Even after he has risen he still says *my brethren*. The word at the empty tomb is the word at the throne, so the least of these are the same people he sends the women to, and the sheep are found to have fed and clothed them.$n$),
(2943,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'matthew',28,10,$n$*Go tell my brethren that they go into Galilee.*$n$,$n$Matthew 28:10 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto them, Be not afraid: go tell my brethren that they go into Galilee, and there shall they see me.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) The word is the same at the empty tomb, where he sends the women to *my brethren* with a promise to meet them. Those whom the goats walked past are people he still claims by that name after he has risen.$n$),
(2944,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'john',20,17,$n$*Go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend.*$n$,$n$John 20:17 — *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto her, Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my Elohim (God), and your Elohim (God).* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) Risen, he sends Mary Magdalene to *my brethren* with a message that gives them his own Father: *my Father, and your Father*. The least of these the sheep served are of that household, with one Father between him and them.$n$),
(2945,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'john',20,17,$n$*Go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend.*$n$,$n$John 20:17 — *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto her, Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my Elohim (God), and your Elohim (God).* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) To the people the goats ignored, the risen one gives his own Father: *my Father, and your Father*. What was refused to the least of them was refused to a member of that household, and that is why he says it was refused to him.$n$),
(2946,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'hebrews',2,11,$n$*He is not ashamed to call them brethren* + Tehillim 22:22 citation.$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:11 — *For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one: for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren,* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) The writer says why he calls them brethren: he who sanctifies and those sanctified are *all of one*. At the throne he is not ashamed to name even the least of them as his own, and the sheep are found to have done for them what they would have done for him.$n$),
(2947,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'hebrews',2,11,$n$*He is not ashamed to call them brethren* + Tehillim 22:22 citation.$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:11 — *For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one: for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren,* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) Because he is *not ashamed to call them brethren*, being *all of one* with them, the want of the least of them is his own want. The sentence to the goats is for leaving his family hungry, and nothing about them is said to have been unknown to him.$n$),
(2948,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'hebrews',2,16,$n$*He took on him the seed of Avraham (Abraham)* — the substantial-Avrahamic-lineage.$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:16 — *For verily he took not on him the nature of angels; but he took on him the seed of Abraham.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) The writer says who he took hold of: *he took on him the seed of Abraham*. The brethren of the throne are that seed, his own people by the fathers' blood, and mercy shown to the least of them is mercy shown to his own flesh.$n$),
(2949,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'hebrews',2,16,$n$*He took on him the seed of Avraham (Abraham)* — the substantial-Avrahamic-lineage.$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:16 — *For verily he took not on him the nature of angels; but he took on him the seed of Abraham.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) He *took on him the seed of Abraham*, so the least of these are kin to him by the fathers' blood and not strangers. The goats withheld from a brother of that line, and he counts it as withheld from himself.$n$),
(2950,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'romans',8,29,$n$*The firstborn among many brethren.*$n$,$n$Romans 8:29 — *For whom he did foreknow, he also did predestinate to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) He is *the firstborn among many brethren*, and the many are those Elohim (God) foreknew. They are the ones the sheep served at the throne, and they were known to the Father long before the throne was set.$n$),
(2951,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'romans',8,29,$n$*The firstborn among many brethren.*$n$,$n$Romans 8:29 — *For whom he did foreknow, he also did predestinate to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) The *firstborn among many brethren* has brethren who were foreknown, and the least of them are among the many. Passing one of them by is passing by a brother of the firstborn, which is why he says it was done not to him.$n$),
(2952,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'deuteronomy',17,15,$n$The Tanakh-anchor *one from among thy brethren* — the king-substance.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 17:15 — *Thou shalt in any wise set him king over thee, whom Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) shall choose: one from among thy brethren shalt thou set king over thee: thou mayest not set a stranger over thee, which is not thy brother.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) Moses laid down that the one set over the people must be *one from among thy brethren*, not a stranger. He who sits on the throne is of their blood, so when he says *my brethren* of the least he speaks as a brother and not as a stranger would.$n$),
(2953,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'deuteronomy',17,15,$n$The Tanakh-anchor *one from among thy brethren* — the king-substance.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 17:15 — *Thou shalt in any wise set him king over thee, whom Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) shall choose: one from among thy brethren shalt thou set king over thee: thou mayest not set a stranger over thee, which is not thy brother.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) The Torah requires that the one set over the people be *one from among thy brethren*, never a stranger, and he is a brother to those he names at the throne. The goats failed one of his own family, and that is how the verse counts it.$n$),
(2954,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'psalms',22,22,$n$*I will declare thy name unto my brethren.*$n$,$n$Psalm 22:22 — *I will declare thy name unto my brethren: in the midst of the congregation will I praise thee.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) The psalm has him say *I will declare thy name unto my brethren*, and at the throne he speaks the same word of the least of them. Those the sheep fed and visited are the people he declares the Father's name to.$n$),
(2955,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'psalms',22,22,$n$*I will declare thy name unto my brethren.*$n$,$n$Psalm 22:22 — *I will declare thy name unto my brethren: in the midst of the congregation will I praise thee.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) He says *I will declare thy name unto my brethren* in the midst of the congregation, and the brethren are his own people. The goats had them before them hungry and thirsty and gave them nothing, and the verse says that was given to him.$n$),
(2956,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'proverbs',17,17,$n$*A brother is born for adversity.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 17:17 — *A friend loveth at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) *A brother is born for adversity*, and the hungry, the sick and the prisoner are in adversity. The sheep stood as brothers to the least in it, and he who calls them *my brethren* counts it as brotherhood shown to him.$n$),
(2957,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'proverbs',17,17,$n$*A brother is born for adversity.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 17:17 — *A friend loveth at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) The proverb says what a brother is for: he is *born for adversity*. The goats were there in the hour of hunger and prison and did not come, and the sentence on the left is for a brotherhood not shown to the least of his brethren.$n$),
(2958,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'acts',9,4,$n$The King identifies-with-his-brethren at-Sha'ul (Saul)'s-persecution-of-the-assembly.$n$,$n$Acts 9:4 — *And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) Saul was dragging men and women out of the way to Jerusalem, and the voice from heaven asks *why persecutest thou me?* He counts what is done to his brethren as done to him, the same weight he gives to the least of these at the throne.$n$),
(2959,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'acts',9,4,$n$The King identifies-with-his-brethren at-Sha'ul (Saul)'s-persecution-of-the-assembly.$n$,$n$Acts 9:4 — *And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) The voice asks *why persecutest thou me?* when Saul had touched only his people, so what is done to them is done to him. Turning away the least of them at the throne is counted the same way: *ye did it not to me*.$n$),
(2960,25,40,$n$And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.$n$,'acts',22,14,$n$*The Elohim (God) of our fathers hath chosen thee* — the substantial-Tanakh-fathers-substance.$n$,$n$Acts 22:14 — *And he said, The Elohim (God) of our fathers hath chosen thee, that thou shouldest know his will, and see that Just One, and shouldest hear the voice of his mouth.* *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, you have done it unto me.* (Matthew 25:40) Paul is told that the Elohim (God) of our fathers chose him to see *that Just One* and hear his voice. The one who calls the least his brethren is the Just One of the fathers' own line, speaking to a son of those fathers.$n$),
(2961,25,45,$n$Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.$n$,'acts',22,14,$n$*The Elohim (God) of our fathers hath chosen thee* — the substantial-Tanakh-fathers-substance.$n$,$n$Acts 22:14 — *And he said, The Elohim (God) of our fathers hath chosen thee, that thou shouldest know his will, and see that Just One, and shouldest hear the voice of his mouth.* *Then shall he answer them, saying, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as you did it not to one of the least of these, you did it not to me.* (Matthew 25:45) Paul heard the voice of *that Just One*, the one who says the least were his. The people the goats passed by belong to the fathers whose Elohim (God) sent him, and that is what makes the neglect weigh on him.$n$),
(2990,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'matthew',25,34,$n$*Inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world.*$n$,$n$Matthew 25:34 — *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, ye blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Against *everlasting punishment* and *life eternal* stands the first word to the right hand, the call to *inherit the kingdom* prepared from the foundation of the world. The inheritance was laid up before any of them lifted a hand, and the two ends bring into the open who kept it.$n$),
(2991,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'matthew',25,46,$n$*Life eternal* — the substantial-millennial-reign-and-beyond-substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 25:46 — *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The kingdom was prepared *from the foundation of the world*, and the last verse says where those who inherit it go: *life eternal*. What the works show was already settled in the Father's preparing, and *everlasting punishment* is the other end of the same sorting.$n$),
(2992,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'isaiah',60,1,$n$*Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) is risen upon thee.* (Isaiah 60:1). Zion arises, and *the Gentiles shall come to thy light* (Isaiah 60:3). The blessed of the Father who *inherit the kingdom* (Matthew 25:34) are the sheep, the gathered seed sifted from the goats; the nations come to their light in the reign.$n$,$n$Isaiah 60:1 — *Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) is risen upon thee.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Zion is told to rise and shine because *thy light is come*. The sheep who inherit are the gathered seed, sifted from the goats, and the glory of Yahuah (the LORD) rises on them, which is the light the nations are drawn to in the reign.$n$),
(2993,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'isaiah',60,1,$n$*Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) is risen upon thee.* (Isaiah 60:1). Zion arises, and *the Gentiles shall come to thy light* (Isaiah 60:3). The righteous who go *into life eternal* (Matthew 25:46) are the sheep, the gathered seed sifted from the goats; the nations come to their light in the reign.$n$,$n$Isaiah 60:1 — *Arise, shine; for thy light is come, and the glory of Yahuah (LORD) is risen upon thee.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) The righteous who go into *life eternal* are the people Zion's call is spoken to: *Arise, shine*. They are the sifted seed with the glory of Yahuah (the LORD) risen on them, and the nations see by their light afterward.$n$),
(2994,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'isaiah',66,18,$n$*For I know their works and their thoughts: it shall come, that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come, and see my glory.* (Isaiah 66:18). The nations come and see his glory in the reign. The inheritance of Matthew 25:34 belongs to the sheep, the gathered seed sifted from the goats — not to the nations who come to see.$n$,$n$Isaiah 66:18 — *For I know their works and their thoughts: it shall come, that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come, and see my glory.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Yahuah (the LORD) knows *their works and their thoughts* and gathers all nations and tongues to see his glory. The inheritance of the sheep belongs to the gathered seed, sifted from the goats, while the nations are gathered to see it.$n$),
(2995,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'isaiah',66,18,$n$*For I know their works and their thoughts: it shall come, that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come, and see my glory.* (Isaiah 66:18). The nations come and see his glory in the reign. The life eternal of Matthew 25:46 belongs to the righteous, the gathered seed sifted from the goats — not to the nations who come to see.$n$,$n$Isaiah 66:18 — *For I know their works and their thoughts: it shall come, that I will gather all nations and tongues; and they shall come, and see my glory.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) The nations and tongues come to *see my glory*, and their works and thoughts are known. The life of the righteous belongs to the sifted seed, and the nations come as those who see, not as those who were sorted at the throne.$n$),
(2996,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'zechariah',14,16,$n$*And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* (Zechariah 14:16). The nations left alive keep the feast in the reign. The blessed who *inherit the kingdom* (Matthew 25:34) are not those nations; they are the gathered seed, sifted from the goats, who enter the land as a kingdom of priests to the nations who are left.$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:16 — *And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Those left of the nations that came against Jerusalem go up every year to keep *the feast of tabernacles*. They are the nations left alive; the sheep who inherit are the gathered seed who minister to them as priests, not those who come up to bow.$n$),
(2997,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'zechariah',14,16,$n$*And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* (Zechariah 14:16). The nations left alive keep the feast in the reign. The righteous of Matthew 25:46 are not those nations; they are the gathered seed, sifted from the goats, who enter the land as a kingdom of priests to the nations who are left.$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:16 — *And it shall come to pass, that every one that is left of all the nations which came against Jerusalem shall even go up from year to year to worship the King, Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts), and to keep the feast of tabernacles.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) The nations left alive go up every year to *keep the feast of tabernacles*. The righteous of Matthew's last verse are not those nations but the gathered seed, sifted from the goats, who enter the land as priests to them.$n$),
(2998,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'revelation',21,24,$n$*And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it.* (Revelation 21:24). The glory brought into the New Jerusalem belongs to the new heaven and the new earth, after the reign and the judgment of the dead. The kingdom the sheep inherit is not the nations' portion; the nations walk in its light.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:24 — *And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) John sees the nations walking *in the light of it* in the new heaven and new earth, after the reign and after the judgment of the dead. The kingdom the sheep inherit comes before that, and John's nations walk by its light and are not the heirs.$n$),
(2999,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'revelation',21,24,$n$*And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it.* (Revelation 21:24). The glory brought into the New Jerusalem belongs to the new heaven and the new earth, after the reign and the judgment of the dead. The righteous of Matthew 25:46 are the sifted seed; the nations walk in the light of the city.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:24 — *And the nations of them which are saved shall walk in the light of it: and the kings of the earth do bring their glory and honour into it.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) In the new heaven and new earth the nations *shall walk in the light of it*, with the kings bringing their glory into it. The righteous of Matthew's last verse are the sifted seed, and the nations are the ones who walk by the city's light.$n$),
(3000,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'revelation',22,1,$n$The leaves-of-the-tree for-the-healing-of-the-nations.$n$,$n$Revelation 22:1 — *And he shewed me a pure river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of Elohim (God) and of the Lamb.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) A river of the water of life runs out of *the throne of Elohim (God) and of the Lamb*, and John adds that *the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations* (Revelation 22:2). Matthew's throne is where the sheep are sorted and told to inherit; John's is the throne at the end.$n$),
(3001,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'revelation',22,1,$n$The tree-of-life + the leaves-for-the-healing-of-the-nations.$n$,$n$Revelation 22:1 — *And he shewed me a pure river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding out of the throne of Elohim (God) and of the Lamb.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) John sees the river of life and the tree beside it, and says *the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations* (Revelation 22:2). The life the righteous go into in Matthew's last verse is shown here as a river and a tree, with the nations healed by its leaves.$n$),
(3002,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'revelation',20,4,$n$The first-resurrection + the millennial-reign-substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:4 — *And I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them: and I saw the souls of them that were beheaded for the witness of Yahusha (Jesus), and for the word of Elohim (God), and which had not worshipped the beast, neither his image, neither had received his mark upon their foreheads, or in their hands; and they lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) John sees thrones, judgment given to those on them, and a company who *lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years*. The kingdom the sheep are called to inherit is a reign, and John counts it at a thousand years.$n$),
(3003,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'revelation',20,4,$n$The first-resurrection + the millennial-reign-substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:4 — *And I saw thrones, and they sat upon them, and judgment was given unto them: and I saw the souls of them that were beheaded for the witness of Yahusha (Jesus), and for the word of Elohim (God), and which had not worshipped the beast, neither his image, neither had received his mark upon their foreheads, or in their hands; and they lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Matthew's last verse names two ends, and John shows the reign of the first: some *lived and reigned with Messiah (Christ) a thousand years*. Matthew names the life of the righteous, and John numbers the reign they live in.$n$),
(3004,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'joel',3,1,$n$*For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem,* (Joel 3:1). Joel's valley of Jehoshaphat is the Day on the nations gathered against his land — *I will also gather all nations... and will plead with them there for my people* (Joel 3:2): the tares abroad burned first. The inheritance of the sheep is another matter: the gathered seed, sifted from the goats before the reign.$n$,$n$Joel 3:1 — *For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem,* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Joel puts the gathering at the time he *shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem*, and the nations gathered after it are judged in the valley. The sheep's inheritance is another matter: it belongs to the gathered seed, sifted from the goats before the reign.$n$),
(3005,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'joel',3,1,$n$*For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem,* (Joel 3:1). Joel's valley of Jehoshaphat is the Day on the nations gathered against his land — *I will also gather all nations... and will plead with them there for my people* (Joel 3:2): the tares abroad burned first. The parting of Matthew 25:46 is among the gathered seed, the goats from the sheep, before the reign.$n$,$n$Joel 3:1 — *For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem,* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Joel speaks of the time he *shall bring again the captivity of Yahudah (Judah) and Jerusalem*, with nations gathered after it. The parting in Matthew's last verse is among the gathered seed, the goats from the sheep, before the reign.$n$),
(3006,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'micah',4,1,$n$The nations flow-unto-the-mountain-of-Yahuah's-house.$n$,$n$Micah 4:1 — *But in the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of Yahuah (LORD) shall be established in the top of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills; and people shall flow unto it.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) In the last days the mountain of the house of Yahuah (the LORD) is set above the hills and *people shall flow unto it*. That is the reign's own scene, with peoples coming up to the mountain, while the kingdom prepared for the sheep is the one they come to.$n$),
(3007,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'micah',4,1,$n$The nations flow-unto-the-mountain-of-Yahuah's-house.$n$,$n$Micah 4:1 — *But in the last days it shall come to pass, that the mountain of the house of Yahuah (LORD) shall be established in the top of the mountains, and it shall be exalted above the hills; and people shall flow unto it.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) The mountain of the house of Yahuah (the LORD) is lifted up in the last days and *people shall flow unto it*. The righteous who go into life are the gathered seed, and the peoples come to the mountain in the reign that follows the sorting.$n$),
(3008,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'isaiah',2,2,$n$The nations come to-the-Father's-house at-the-substantial-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 2:2 — *And it shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’S house shall be established in the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills; and all nations shall flow unto it.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Isaiah says *all nations shall flow unto it*, the mountain of the house of Yahuah (the LORD) lifted up in the last days. The nations come to the mountain; the sheep are told to inherit the kingdom prepared for them, and that is a different place in the story.$n$),
(3009,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'isaiah',2,2,$n$The nations come to-the-Father's-house at-the-substantial-substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 2:2 — *And it shall come to pass in the last days, that the mountain of the LORD’S house shall be established in the top of the mountains, and shall be exalted above the hills; and all nations shall flow unto it.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Isaiah sees the mountain of the house of Yahuah (the LORD) exalted, and *all nations shall flow unto it*. The righteous who go into *life eternal* are the sifted seed, and the nations come up to the mountain in the days of the reign.$n$),
(4088,25,1,$n$Then shall the kingdom of heaven be likened unto ten virgins, which took their lamps, and went forth to meet the bridegroom.$n$,'luke',12,35,$n$*Let your loins be girded about, and your lights burning.*$n$,$n$Luke 12:35 — *Let your loins be girded about, and your lights burning;* *Then shall the kingdom of heaven be likened unto ten virgins, which took their lamps, and went forth to meet the bridegroom.* (Matthew 25:1) Luke gives the same word to the household that waits for its master: *Let your loins be girded about, and your lights burning*. Matthew's ten virgins take lamps and go out to meet the bridegroom, all of them of the house, and the lights are what the five foolish ones let go out.$n$),
(4089,25,31,$n$When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:$n$,'zechariah',14,5,$n$*Yahuah (the LORD) my Elohim (God) shall come, and all the saints with thee.*$n$,$n$Zechariah 14:5 — *And ye shall flee to the valley of the mountains; for the valley of the mountains shall reach unto Azal: yea, ye shall flee, like as ye fled from before the earthquake in the days of Uzziah king of Yahudah (Judah): and Yahuah Elohai (the LORD my God) shall come, and all the saints with thee.* *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31) Zechariah says his Elohim (God) shall come with all the saints, and Matthew has the Son of Adam come with *all the holy angels with him*. The one who comes brings his holy ones, and Zechariah says it is Yahuah (the LORD) himself coming.$n$),
(4090,25,31,$n$When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:$n$,'deuteronomy',33,2,$n$*He came with ten thousands of saints.*$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 33:2 — *And he said, Yahuah (LORD) came from Sinai, and rose up from Seir unto them; he shined forth from mount Paran, and he came with ten thousands of saints: from his right hand went a fiery law for them.* *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31) *Yahuah (LORD) came from Sinai* with *ten thousands of saints*, and from his right hand went a fiery law. The one who came to the mountain with his holy ones is the one who comes with all the holy angels and sits on the throne of his glory.$n$),
(4091,25,31,$n$When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:$n$,'2-thessalonians',1,7,$n$*Revealed from heaven with his mighty angels.*$n$,$n$2 Thessalonians 1:7 — *And to you who are troubled rest with us, when the Lord Yahusha (Lord Jesus) shall be revealed from heaven with his mighty angels,* *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31) Paul says rest comes to the troubled when Yahusha (Jesus) is *revealed from heaven with his mighty angels*. It is the coming Matthew sets before the throne, with the holy angels about him, and the relief belongs to those who have been troubled.$n$),
(4092,25,31,$n$When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:$n$,'revelation',19,11,$n$The King-and-the-armies-of-heaven.$n$,$n$Revelation 19:11 — *And I saw heaven opened, and behold a white horse; and he that sat upon him was called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he doth judge and make war.* *When the Son of Adam shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory:* (Matthew 25:31) John sees heaven opened and one called *Faithful and True*, who judges in righteousness, and Matthew sets the Son of Adam on the throne with his angels. Both show the coming one as judge, and John's verse names him by what he is.$n$),
(4093,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'matthew',12,46,$n$The King's-brethren = those-who-do-the-will-of-the-Father.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:46 — *While he yet talked to the people, behold, his mother and his brethren stood without, desiring to speak with him.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Those on the right hand are called *blessed of my Father*, and when his mother and brethren stand outside he points to his disciples with *Behold my mother and my brethren!* (Matthew 12:49). The Father's blessed are the ones he claims as family.$n$),
(4094,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'hebrews',2,11,$n$*He is not ashamed to call them brethren* + the seed-of-Avraham.$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:11 — *For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one: for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren,* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The one who says *Come, ye blessed of my Father* is *not ashamed to call them brethren*, for he and they are *all of one*. The inheritance is given to the sheep as family, before any of them did a work.$n$),
(4095,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'romans',8,29,$n$*The firstborn among many brethren.*$n$,$n$Romans 8:29 — *For whom he did foreknow, he also did predestinate to be conformed to the image of his Son, that he might be the firstborn among many brethren.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Paul says *whom he did foreknow* are the brethren of the firstborn, and Matthew says the kingdom was *prepared for you from the foundation of the world*. The inheritance was settled before anyone fed anyone, and the works at the throne show who those were.$n$),
(4096,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'isaiah',58,6,$n$The Tanakh-substance of the fast-the-Father-chose.$n$,$n$Isaiah 58:6 — *Is not this the fast that I have chosen? to loose the bands of wickedness, to undo the heavy burdens, and to let the oppressed go free, and that ye break every yoke?* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The fast Yahuah (the LORD) chose is *to loose the bands of wickedness*, to undo burdens and let the oppressed go free. The works named at the throne are mercy the Torah and the prophets already ask for, and not a new standard put in its place.$n$),
(4097,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'isaiah',61,1,$n$The substantial-Messianic-credential-substance — open-prisons.$n$,$n$Isaiah 61:1 — *The Spirit of Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD) is upon me; because Yahuah (LORD) hath anointed me to preach good tidings unto the meek; he hath sent me to bind up the brokenhearted, to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound;* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The anointed one was sent to *proclaim liberty to the captives*, and the opening of the prison to the bound. At the throne the prisoner who is visited is one of those he came for, and the sheep did what his anointing was for.$n$),
(4098,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'job',31,16,$n$The Tanakh-substance of the care-of-the-vulnerable.$n$,$n$Job 31:16 — *If I have withheld the poor from their desire, or have caused the eyes of the widow to fail;* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Job swears he never *withheld the poor from their desire*, judging himself by the poor and the widow long before the throne. The standard the sheep are found to have kept at the throne is one Job already held himself to, and it is old.$n$),
(4099,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'proverbs',19,17,$n$*He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto Yahuah.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 19:17 — *He that hath pity upon the poor lendeth unto Yahuah (LORD); and that which he hath given will he pay him again.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The proverb says pity on the poor *lendeth unto Yahuah (LORD)*, and what he has given will be paid back. Matthew puts the loan and the paying back at the throne, where what was done for the least of them was done for him.$n$),
(4100,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'deuteronomy',10,18,$n$*Love ye therefore the stranger.*$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 10:18 — *He doth execute the judgment of the fatherless and widow, and loveth the stranger, in giving him food and raiment.* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) Yahuah (the LORD) himself *loveth the stranger, in giving him food and raiment*, and the sheep gave the stranger a welcome and the naked clothes. The mercy at the throne is the mercy Moses said the Father does.$n$),
(4101,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'john',20,17,$n$*Go to my brethren* — direct King-identification.$n$,$n$John 20:17 — *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto her, Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my Elohim (God), and your Elohim (God).* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The one who says *Come, ye blessed of my Father* later says *my Father, and your Father*, and sends word to *my brethren*. He calls them his family at the throne and he calls them so again once he has risen.$n$),
(4102,25,34,$n$Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:$n$,'acts',9,4,$n$*Why persecutest thou me?* — the King identifies with his brethren.$n$,$n$Acts 9:4 — *And he fell to the earth, and heard a voice saying unto him, Saul, Saul, why persecutest thou me?* *Then shall the King say unto them on his right hand, Come, you blessed of my Father, inherit the kingdom prepared for you from the foundation of the world:* (Matthew 25:34) The voice to Saul asks *why persecutest thou me?* so what is done to his people is counted as done to him. The ones on the right are called blessed for what they did to his brethren, and the same reckoning stands.$n$),
(4103,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'revelation',20,10,$n$The devil-cast-into-the-lake-of-fire.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:10 — *And the devil that deceived them was cast into the lake of fire and brimstone, where the beast and the false prophet are, and shall be tormented day and night for ever and ever.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) Matthew says the fire was *prepared for the devil and his angels*, and John sees the devil himself cast into the lake of fire, where the beast and the false prophet already are. The fire was made for him, and those on the left go toward it by the side they stood on.$n$),
(4104,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'revelation',20,14,$n$The second-death substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:14 — *And death and hell were cast into the lake of fire. This is the second death.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) Matthew says everlasting fire, and John calls the lake of fire *the second death*, into which even death and hell are thrown. John says what the fire is, and Matthew says whom it was prepared for.$n$),
(4105,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'2-peter',2,4,$n$The angels-that-sinned cast-down.$n$,$n$2 Peter 2:4 — *For if Elohim (God) spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment;* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) Peter says Elohim (God) *spared not the angels that sinned*, and cast them down to *chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment*. These are the angels in Matthew's *devil and his angels*, the ones the fire was prepared for, and men go there only by taking their side.$n$),
(4106,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'jude',1,6,$n$The angels-which-kept-not-their-first-estate.$n$,$n$Jude 1:6 — *And the angels which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation, he hath reserved in everlasting chains under darkness unto the judgment of the great day.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) The angels *which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation* are held in chains until the great day. The fire was prepared for such as these, the ones who left their place, and men go into it by choosing to be with them.$n$),
(4107,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'proverbs',21,13,$n$*Whoso stoppeth his ears at the cry of the poor.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 21:13 — *Whoso stoppeth his ears at the cry of the poor, he also shall cry himself, but shall not be heard.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) The proverb has the turn the left hand has: the man who stops his ears at *the cry of the poor* cries himself and is not heard. The goats are told to depart, they who gave no meat or drink when the poor cried.$n$),
(4108,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'james',4,17,$n$*To him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not.*$n$,$n$James 4:17 — *Therefore to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) No theft or violence is charged to the left hand, only good that was left undone, and James says it is sin for the one *that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not*. The goats are judged for what they knew and did not do.$n$),
(4109,25,41,$n$Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:$n$,'john',3,16,$n$The love-substance walked-not-in-word-but-in-deed-and-in-truth.$n$,$n$John 3:16 — *For Elohim (God) so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life.* *Then shall he say also unto them on the left hand, Depart from me, you cursed, into everlasting fire, prepared for the devil and his angels:* (Matthew 25:41) In John the Father *so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son*, and in Matthew the hand that gives nothing hears *Depart from me*. The Father's giving is set against the goats' withholding, and *perish* is where that road ends.$n$),
(4110,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'daniel',12,2,$n$*Some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt.*$n$,$n$Daniel 12:2 — *And many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Daniel's two ends, *some to everlasting life, and some to shame and everlasting contempt*, are for the sleepers who awake. Matthew's last verse names two ends of its own, *everlasting punishment* and *life eternal*, and each has the same shape: a life that lasts and a shame that lasts.$n$),
(4111,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'isaiah',66,24,$n$*Their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 66:24 — *And they shall go forth, and look upon the carcases of the men that have transgressed against me: for their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched; and they shall be an abhorring unto all flesh.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) Isaiah says of the men who transgressed that *their worm shall not die, neither shall their fire be quenched*, and they are an abhorring to all flesh. Matthew's *everlasting punishment* is the same kind of lasting end, and Isaiah says it is looked on by those who go forth.$n$),
(4112,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'revelation',20,11,$n$*And I saw a great white throne, and him that sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away; and there was found no place for them.* (Revelation 20:11). *These shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal* (Matthew 25:46) names where each way ends. The sifting of the gathered comes before the reign; the great white throne comes after it, at the judgment of the dead.$n$,$n$Revelation 20:11 — *And I saw a great white throne, and him that sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away; and there was found no place for them.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) The throne Matthew describes is set at his coming, with the gathered sorted first. John's *great white throne* is seen after the thousand years, with earth and heaven fleeing from the face of the one on it, and the two sittings are not the same.$n$),
(4113,25,46,$n$And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.$n$,'revelation',21,1,$n$The new-heavens-and-new-earth substance.$n$,$n$Revelation 21:1 — *And I saw a new heaven and a new earth: for the first heaven and the first earth were passed away; and there was no more sea.* *And these shall go away into everlasting punishment: but the righteous into life eternal.* (Matthew 25:46) John sees *a new heaven and a new earth*, when the first heaven and earth have passed away. Matthew's last verse names the life of the righteous at the throne, and John shows what the first heaven and earth give way to.$n$),
(3065,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'exodus',12,1,$n$The Tanakh-Pesach-substance.$n$,$n$Exodus 12:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt, saying,* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Yahusha (Jesus) names the feast and his own betrayal in one breath, and the feast is the one Yahuah (the LORD) gave to Moses and Aaron in Egypt. Matthew sets his death inside the passover Moses received, and nothing in either verse sets that feast aside.$n$),
(3066,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'exodus',12,1,$n$The Tanakh-Pesach.$n$,$n$Exodus 12:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt, saying,* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) Yahusha (Jesus) does not set the feast aside: he has his disciples prepare it and says *I will keep the passover at thy house with my disciples* (Matthew 26:18). The keeping goes back to where Yahuah (the LORD) first spoke it to Moses and Aaron in Egypt, and he keeps it as it was given.$n$),
(3067,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'john',1,29,$n$*Behold the Lamb of Elohim.*$n$,$n$John 1:29 — *The next day John seeth Yahusha (Jesus) coming unto him, and saith, Behold the Lamb of Elohim (God), which taketh away the sin of the world.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Two days before the feast he says he will be betrayed to be crucified. John the Baptist had already pointed at him and said *Behold the Lamb of Elohim (God)*, and the feast he names is the one in which a lamb is killed.$n$),
(3068,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'john',1,36,$n$*Behold the Lamb of Elohim.*$n$,$n$John 1:36 — *And looking upon Yahusha (Jesus) as he walked, he saith, Behold the Lamb of Elohim (God)!* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples ask where to prepare the passover, which means a lamb. John the Baptist had pointed at Yahusha (Jesus) as he walked and said *Behold the Lamb of Elohim (God)!*, so the lamb of the feast and the one who sits down to eat it are named together.$n$),
(3069,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'john',19,14,$n$The substantial-substance the *not a bone of him broken* per Shemot 12:46.$n$,$n$John 19:14 — *And it was the preparation of the passover, and about the sixth hour: and he saith unto the Yahudim (Jews), Behold your King!* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Two days before, he said the feast was near and he would be betrayed to be crucified. In John the feast has come: *the preparation of the passover*, and he is brought out and shown to the Yahudim (Jews) with the words *Behold your King!*$n$),
(3070,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'john',19,36,$n$The substantial-substance the *not a bone of him broken* per Shemot 12:46.$n$,$n$John 19:36 — *For these things were done, that the scripture should be fulfilled, A bone of him shall not be broken.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The preparation Matthew begins here ends at the cross in John, where the things done are said to be done *that the scripture should be fulfilled*, and the words are *A bone of him shall not be broken*. It is the care the passover lamb was given in Egypt, now laid on the one the disciples are preparing the meal for.$n$),
(3071,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'1-corinthians',5,7,$n$*Messiah our passover is sacrificed for us.*$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 5:7 — *Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Messiah (Christ) our passover is sacrificed for us:* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Paul speaks of the feast as a feast that stands: the old leaven is purged and the unleavened lump is kept. In the middle of it he says *Messiah (Christ) our passover is sacrificed for us*, which is the lamb of the feast Yahusha (Jesus) names two days ahead.$n$),
(3072,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'1-corinthians',5,7,$n$*Messiah our passover is sacrificed for us.*$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 5:7 — *Purge out therefore the old leaven, that ye may be a new lump, as ye are unleavened. For even Messiah (Christ) our passover is sacrificed for us:* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples ask where to prepare the passover, and Paul writes that *Messiah (Christ) our passover is sacrificed for us*, naming the lamb. He goes on in the next verse, *Therefore let us keep the feast* (1 Corinthians 5:8), so the feast stays and its lamb has been named.$n$),
(3073,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'1-peter',1,18,$n$The precious-blood as-of-a-lamb-without-blemish-and-without-spot.$n$,$n$1 Peter 1:18 — *Forasmuch as ye know that ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold, from your vain conversation received by tradition from your fathers;* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Peter says what the price was not, *silver and gold*, and a verse on says what it was, the blood of Messiah (Christ), *as of a lamb without blemish and without spot* (1 Peter 1:19). That is the kind of lamb the passover called for, and Matthew's two days are the count down to it.$n$),
(3074,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'1-peter',1,18,$n$The precious-blood as-of-a-lamb-without-blemish-and-without-spot.$n$,$n$1 Peter 1:18 — *Forasmuch as ye know that ye were not redeemed with corruptible things, as silver and gold, from your vain conversation received by tradition from your fathers;* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) They are preparing the passover meal, and Peter says redemption did not come by *silver and gold* but by the blood of Messiah (Christ), *as of a lamb without blemish and without spot* (1 Peter 1:19). A lamb without blemish is how the passover lamb was chosen.$n$),
(3075,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'revelation',5,6,$n$The Lamb-as-it-had-been-slain.$n$,$n$Revelation 5:6 — *And I beheld, and, lo, in the midst of the throne and of the four beasts, and in the midst of the elders, stood a Lamb as it had been slain, having seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of Elohim (God) sent forth into all the earth.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) In the same breath as the feast he says he is to be betrayed and crucified, and Revelation shows the lamb standing in the midst of the throne *as it had been slain*. The one given up at the passover is the one John sees still carrying the mark of it.$n$),
(3076,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'revelation',5,12,$n$The Lamb-as-it-had-been-slain.$n$,$n$Revelation 5:12 — *Saying with a loud voice, Worthy is the Lamb that was slain to receive power, and riches, and wisdom, and strength, and honour, and glory, and blessing.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples ask where to eat the passover, and in Revelation the voices cry *Worthy is the Lamb that was slain*. The lamb of the table in Matthew and the Lamb in John's vision are the same lamb, now given power, riches, wisdom, strength, honour, glory and blessing.$n$),
(3077,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'revelation',7,14,$n$The robes-washed-in-the-blood-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 7:14 — *And I said unto him, Sir, thou knowest. And he said to me, These are they which came out of great tribulation, and have washed their robes, and made them white in the blood of the Lamb.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Matthew gives the day he is betrayed to be crucified, and Revelation shows who stands before the throne because of it. They came out of great tribulation and have *made them white in the blood of the Lamb*, so the blood begun at the feast is still washing.$n$),
(3078,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'revelation',7,14,$n$The robes-washed-in-the-blood-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 7:14 — *And I said unto him, Sir, thou knowest. And he said to me, These are they which came out of great tribulation, and have washed their robes, and made them white in the blood of the Lamb.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) Matthew shows the disciples preparing the passover for the one whose blood is to be shed, and Revelation shows what that blood does. Those who came out of great tribulation have washed their robes and *made them white in the blood of the Lamb*.$n$),
(3079,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'revelation',12,11,$n$They-overcame-him-by-the-blood-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 12:11 — *And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony; and they loved not their lives unto the death.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Yahusha (Jesus) says he is to be betrayed and crucified at the feast, and Revelation says what the lamb's blood did. The accuser of the brethren was overcome *by the blood of the Lamb*, and by *the word of their testimony*, and those who overcame him *loved not their lives unto the death*.$n$),
(3080,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'revelation',12,11,$n$They-overcame-him-by-the-blood-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 12:11 — *And they overcame him by the blood of the Lamb, and by the word of their testimony; and they loved not their lives unto the death.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples prepare the passover and Revelation says what the Lamb's blood has done: the accuser was overcome *by the blood of the Lamb* and *the word of their testimony*. The ones who overcame *loved not their lives unto the death*, as the Lamb gave his.$n$),
(3081,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$*He is brought as a lamb to the slaughter.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Two days before the feast he says he will be betrayed to be crucified, and Isaiah had already described the man who goes to it without a word. *Brought as a lamb to the slaughter*, he opens not his mouth, and the feast he names is the one that kills a lamb.$n$),
(3082,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$*He is brought as a lamb to the slaughter.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples ask where to prepare the passover, and Isaiah had described the lamb long before: *he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter*, and as a sheep before her shearers he opens not his mouth. The one eating the meal is the one brought to it silent.$n$),
(3083,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'genesis',22,8,$n$*Elohim will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering.*$n$,$n$Genesis 22:8 — *And Abraham said, My son, Elohim (God) will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering: so they went both of them together.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Abraham, going up the mountain with his son, said *Elohim (God) will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering*. Read beside Matthew, the lamb that Elohim (God) provides for himself is standing at the table two days before the passover, saying he will be betrayed to be crucified.$n$),
(3084,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'genesis',22,8,$n$*Elohim will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering.*$n$,$n$Genesis 22:8 — *And Abraham said, My son, Elohim (God) will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering: so they went both of them together.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) On the first day of unleavened bread the disciples ask where the lamb is to be prepared. Abraham's answer to his son long before was *Elohim (God) will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering*, and the lamb at this table is the one Elohim (God) provided.$n$),
(3085,26,2,$n$You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.$n$,'revelation',19,7,$n$The marriage-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 19:7 — *Let us be glad and rejoice, and give honour to him: for the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready.* *You know that after two days is the feast of the passover, and the Son of Adam is betrayed to be crucified.* (Matthew 26:2) Revelation closes the lamb's story at a wedding, *the marriage of the Lamb is come*, and his wife has made herself ready. Matthew begins it at the passover with Yahusha (Jesus) saying he will be betrayed to be crucified, so the same lamb runs from one verse to the other.$n$),
(3086,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'revelation',19,7,$n$The marriage-of-the-Lamb.$n$,$n$Revelation 19:7 — *Let us be glad and rejoice, and give honour to him: for the marriage of the Lamb is come, and his wife hath made herself ready.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The disciples prepare the passover meal and Revelation ends at a wedding feast, *the marriage of the Lamb is come*, and his wife has made herself ready. The lamb who sits at this table is the one the feast in Revelation is named for.$n$),
(4114,26,1,$n$And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,$n$,'matthew',21,33,$n$The wicked-husbandmen substance — the precursor of the conspiracy.$n$,$n$Matthew 21:33 — *Hear another parable: There was a certain householder, which planted a vineyard, and hedged it round about, and digged a winepress in it, and built a tower, and let it out to husbandmen, and went into a far country:* *And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,* (Matthew 26:1) These are the sayings Yahusha (Jesus) has just finished, and this parable is among them: a householder lets his vineyard out to husbandmen, and when his servants come for the fruit they are beaten and killed. The plot that opens in the verses after this one is that parable being acted out.$n$),
(4115,26,1,$n$And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,$n$,'matthew',23,34,$n$The seven-woes substance the King-substance walked-out.$n$,$n$Matthew 23:34 — *Wherefore, behold, I send unto you prophets, and wise men, and scribes: and some of them ye shall kill and crucify; and some of them shall ye scourge in your synagogues, and persecute them from city to city:* *And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,* (Matthew 26:1) He had told the leaders to their faces what was coming: *some of them ye shall kill and crucify*. In the next verses the chief priests and elders gather at the high priest's palace to consult how to kill him, so the plot follows the warning and does not surprise him.$n$),
(4116,26,1,$n$And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,$n$,'psalms',2,1,$n$*The kings of the earth ... take counsel together, against Yahuah, and against his anointed.*$n$,$n$Psalm 2:1 — *Why do the heathen rage, and the people imagine a vain thing?* *And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,* (Matthew 26:1) The psalm asks the question the next verses answer. In Matthew the chief priests, scribes and elders gather and consult against Yahusha (Jesus), and the psalm had already pictured the rulers who *take counsel together, against Yahuah (LORD), and against his anointed* (Psalm 2:2).$n$),
(4117,26,1,$n$And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,$n$,'john',11,47,$n$The Sanhedrin-substance' previous-substantial-counsel.$n$,$n$John 11:47 — *Then gathered the chief priests and the Pharisees a council, and said, What do we? for this man doeth many miracles.* *And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,* (Matthew 26:1) This is the council behind Matthew's council. John shows the chief priests and Pharisees first gathering because *this man doeth many miracles*, and Caiaphas, the high priest that year, is already saying that it is expedient for one man to die for the people.$n$),
(4118,26,1,$n$And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,$n$,'exodus',12,1,$n$The Tanakh-Pesach-substance.$n$,$n$Exodus 12:1 — *And Yahuah (LORD) spake unto Moses and Aaron in the land of Egypt, saying,* *And it came to pass, when Yahusha (Jesus) had finished all these sayings, he said unto his disciples,* (Matthew 26:1) Right after the sayings are finished, the first thing he speaks of is the passover, and the passover begins where this verse begins, with Yahuah (the LORD) speaking to Moses and Aaron in Egypt. The feast he names two days ahead is the one Moses was given.$n$),
(4119,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'john',12,1,$n$The Yochanan-parallel + Miryam-of-Beit-Anyah-named.$n$,$n$John 12:1 — *Then Yahusha (Jesus) six days before the passover came to Bethany, where Lazarus was which had been dead, whom he raised from the dead.* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) John dates this supper for us, *six days before the passover*, and puts it at Bethany with Lazarus at the table. In John the woman is Mary and the ointment goes on his feet, and Judas Iscariot is the one who complains; Matthew has it poured on his head with the disciples complaining.$n$),
(4120,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'luke',7,36,$n$The sinful-woman-anointing — different-event-substance.$n$,$n$Luke 7:36 — *And one of the Pharisees desired him that he would eat with him. And he went into the Pharisee’s house, and sat down to meat.* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) Luke has a woman with an alabaster box at a meal too, but it is in a Pharisee's house, she is weeping at his feet, and the objection is about who she is. Matthew's meal is in the house of Simon the leper and the objection is about waste, so these read as two different suppers.$n$),
(4121,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'1-samuel',10,1,$n$The anointing-of-the-king.$n$,$n$1 Samuel 10:1 — *Then Samuel took a vial of oil, and poured it upon his head, and kissed him, and said, Is it not because Yahuah (LORD) hath anointed thee to be captain over his inheritance?* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) When Samuel took his vial of oil and poured it on a head, it set a man apart as captain over Yahuah's (the LORD's) inheritance. A woman pours oil on Yahusha's (Jesus's) head at Bethany, and the old anointing of a king sits beside her. He says what he saw in it: *she did it for my burial* (Matthew 26:12).$n$),
(4122,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'1-samuel',16,13,$n$The anointing-of-David.$n$,$n$1 Samuel 16:13 — *Then Samuel took the horn of oil, and anointed him in the midst of his brethren: and the Spirit of Yahuah (LORD) came upon David from that day forward. So Samuel rose up, and went to Ramah.* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) Samuel's horn of oil on David's head, in the midst of his brethren, was the anointing of the one Yahuah (the LORD) had chosen, and the Spirit came upon David from that day. Oil on Yahusha's (Jesus's) head at Bethany reads line on line with it, and he names a second meaning, his burial.$n$),
(4123,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'psalms',45,7,$n$*Therefore Elohim (God), thy Elohim (God), hath anointed thee.*$n$,$n$Psalm 45:7 — *Thou lovest righteousness, and hatest wickedness: therefore Elohim (God), thy Elohim (God), hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows.* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) The psalm speaks to a king whose throne is *for ever and ever* (Psalm 45:6) and who is anointed *with the oil of gladness above thy fellows*. The woman pours precious ointment on Yahusha's (Jesus's) head while the disciples count the cost, and the psalm's anointing stands behind what she did.$n$),
(4124,26,6,$n$Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,$n$,'deuteronomy',15,11,$n$*The poor shall never cease out of the land.*$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 15:11 — *For the poor shall never cease out of the land: therefore I command thee, saying, Thou shalt open thine hand wide unto thy brother, to thy poor, and to thy needy, in thy land.* *Now when Yahusha (Jesus) was in Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper,* (Matthew 26:6) The disciples say the ointment should have been given to the poor, and Yahusha (Jesus) answers with Moses' own words, *ye have the poor always with you* (Matthew 26:11). The commandment in that verse to open the hand wide to the poor is not set aside; he is naming one hour that will not come again.$n$),
(4125,26,14,$n$Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went unto the chief priests,$n$,'john',13,2,$n$The substantial-substance the devil-substance walks-into-Yahudah.$n$,$n$John 13:2 — *And supper being ended, the devil having now put into the heart of Judas Iscariot, Simon’s son, to betray him;* *Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went unto the chief priests,* (Matthew 26:14) Matthew gives Judas's walk to the chief priests. John says what had been put into his heart first, *the devil having now put into the heart of Judas Iscariot*, the thought to betray. He still went and asked *What will ye give me, and I will deliver him unto you* (Matthew 26:15), so the choice and the price were his.$n$),
(4126,26,14,$n$Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went unto the chief priests,$n$,'john',13,27,$n$The substantial-substance the devil-substance walks-into-Yahudah.$n$,$n$John 13:27 — *And after the sop Satan entered into him. Then said Yahusha (Jesus) unto him, That thou doest, do quickly.* *Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went unto the chief priests,* (Matthew 26:14) John shows the table behind Matthew's bargain. Yahusha (Jesus) gives Judas the sop, *Satan entered into him*, and Judas is told *That thou doest, do quickly*. The thirty pieces were already agreed, and now Judas goes out, and it was night.$n$),
(4127,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'mark',14,12,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 14:12 — *And the first day of unleavened bread, when they killed the passover, his disciples said unto him, Where wilt thou that we go and prepare that thou mayest eat the passover?* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) Mark tells the same question the same way and adds what kind of day it was, *when they killed the passover*. The disciples ask about preparing the meal on the day the lambs were slain, so the supper and the lamb come together in one day.$n$),
(4128,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'luke',22,7,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 22:7 — *Then came the day of unleavened bread, when the passover must be killed.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) Luke gives the same day and says it as a requirement, *the passover must be killed*. The disciples ask where to prepare it because the day itself asked for it, and Yahusha (Jesus) keeps the feast as the day required.$n$),
(4129,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'1-corinthians',11,23,$n$The Pauline-tradition.$n$,$n$1 Corinthians 11:23 — *For I have received of Yahusha (the Lord) that which also I delivered unto you, That Yahusha (the Lord Jesus) the same night in which he was betrayed took bread:* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) Matthew has the disciples preparing the passover, and Paul says what happened at that meal, that *the same night in which he was betrayed* Yahusha (Jesus) took bread. Paul says he received it from Yahusha (Jesus) and handed it on as it was given.$n$),
(4130,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'exodus',24,8,$n$*Behold the blood of the covenant.*$n$,$n$Exodus 24:8 — *And Moses took the blood, and sprinkled it on the people, and said, Behold the blood of the covenant, which Yahuah (LORD) hath made with you concerning all these words.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) At the table he will say *this is my blood of the new testament* (Matthew 26:28), and Moses had used almost the same words when he sprinkled the people, *Behold the blood of the covenant*. That covenant was made *concerning all these words*, the commandments, so the words at the table are Moses' own.$n$),
(4131,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'jeremiah',31,31,$n$The new-covenant Tanakh-anchor.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 31:31 — *Behold, the days come, saith Yahuah (LORD), that I will make a new covenant with the house of Yashar'el (Israel), and with the house of Yahudah (Judah):* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The new covenant has an address on it already: *with the house of Yashar'el (Israel)* and *the house of Yahudah (Judah)*, both houses. Two verses on Yahuah (the LORD) says how it is kept, *I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts* (Jeremiah 31:33), the commandments written inside.$n$),
(4132,26,17,$n$Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?$n$,'isaiah',53,10,$n$The ransom-for-many substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:10 — *Yet it pleased Yahuah (LORD) to bruise him; he hath put him to grief: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin, he shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days, and the pleasure of Yahuah (LORD) shall prosper in his hand.* *Now the first day of the feast of unleavened bread the disciples came to Yahusha (Jesus), saying unto him, Where will you that we prepare for you to eat the passover?* (Matthew 26:17) The cup is *shed for many* (Matthew 26:28), and Isaiah says who the many are. The servant's soul is made an offering for sin, *he shall see his seed*, and *he shall bear their iniquities* (Isaiah 53:11). The many are the seed he sees.$n$),
(4133,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'mark',14,43,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 14:43 — *And immediately, while he yet spake, cometh Judas, one of the twelve, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and the scribes and the elders.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) Mark tells the arrest in nearly the same words and adds the scribes to those who sent the crowd. In both, Judas comes while Yahusha (Jesus) is still speaking, and the swords and staves are sent by the chief priests and elders, not raised by the people.$n$),
(4134,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'luke',22,47,$n$The Lukan-parallel + the substantial-substance-of-the-King's-healing-of-the-ear.$n$,$n$Luke 22:47 — *And while he yet spake, behold a multitude, and he that was called Judas, one of the twelve, went before them, and drew near unto Yahusha (Jesus) to kiss him.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) Luke has Judas walking *before them* to kiss him, and gives two things Matthew leaves out. Yahusha (Jesus) says *Judas, betrayest thou the Son of Adam with a kiss?* (Luke 22:48), and when the ear is cut off he touches it and heals him (Luke 22:51).$n$),
(4135,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'john',18,2,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + Malkos-named.$n$,$n$John 18:2 — *And Judas also, which betrayed him, knew the place: for Yahusha (Jesus) ofttimes resorted thither with his disciples.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) John says why Judas could lead them, he *knew the place*, because Yahusha (Jesus) went there often with his disciples. John also shows him not caught off guard, but *knowing all things that should come upon him* (John 18:4), he goes out and asks *Whom seek ye?* (John 18:4)$n$),
(4136,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'proverbs',27,6,$n$*The kisses of an enemy are deceitful.*$n$,$n$Proverbs 27:6 — *Faithful are the wounds of a friend; but the kisses of an enemy are deceitful.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) Judas arrives at the head of the crowd, and the sign he gave them was a kiss (Matthew 26:48). Proverbs already knew the two kinds of touch, *the wounds of a friend* and *the kisses of an enemy*, and this kiss, from one who ate at the table, belongs to the second kind.$n$),
(4137,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'psalms',55,12,$n$The familiar-friend substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 55:12 — *For it was not an enemy that reproached me; then I could have borne it: neither was it he that hated me that did magnify himself against me; then I would have hid myself from him:* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) David's psalm describes a betrayal that hurt because it was no stranger's: *it was thou, a man mine equal, my guide, and mine acquaintance* (Psalm 55:13). Judas comes to Yahusha (Jesus) with the crowd, one of the twelve, and is still called *Friend* (Matthew 26:50).$n$),
(4138,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'genesis',9,6,$n$*Whoso sheddeth man's blood, by man shall his blood be shed.*$n$,$n$Genesis 9:6 — *Whoso sheddeth man’s blood, by man shall his blood be shed: for in the image of Elohim (God) made he man.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) Genesis gives the measure of the sword: blood shed by man is answered by man. When a disciple draws his sword in the garden Yahusha (Jesus) says *all they that take the sword shall perish with the sword* (Matthew 26:52), and tells him to put it up. He refuses the sword to save himself.$n$),
(4139,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'zechariah',13,7,$n$*Smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered.*$n$,$n$Zechariah 13:7 — *Awake, O sword, against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow, saith Yahuah Tseva'ot (LORD of hosts): smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered: and I will turn mine hand upon the little ones.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) Yahusha (Jesus) had told them at the mount of Olives that it is written *I will smite the shepherd* (Matthew 26:31), and now the crowd with swords and staves has come. Zechariah puts the sword's awaking in the mouth of Yahuah (the LORD), and in Matthew *all the disciples forsook him, and fled* (Matthew 26:56), the sheep scattered.$n$),
(4140,26,47,$n$And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.$n$,'john',10,17,$n$*No man taketh it from me, but I lay it down of myself.*$n$,$n$John 10:17 — *Therefore doth my Father love me, because I lay down my life, that I might take it again.* *And while he yet spake, lo, Judas, one of the twelve, came, and with him a great multitude with swords and staves, from the chief priests and elders of the people.* (Matthew 26:47) He could ask the Father for *more than twelve legions of angels* (Matthew 26:53), and he does not. John gives the reason in his own words, he lays down his life, and a verse on, *No man taketh it from me, but I lay it down of myself* (John 10:18), a charge he received from his Father.$n$),
(4141,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'mark',14,53,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 14:53 — *And they led Yahusha (Jesus) away to the high priest: and with him were assembled all the chief priests and the elders and the scribes.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Mark tells it the same way and adds the full list around the high priest, *all the chief priests and the elders and the scribes* were assembled. The men who sit to judge him are the whole court, and Mark says in the next verses they sought witness and found none.$n$),
(4142,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'luke',22,54,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 22:54 — *Then took they him, and led him, and brought him into the high priest’s house. And Peter followed afar off.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Luke has the same walk to the high priest's house and puts Peter in the same sentence, *Peter followed afar off*, as Matthew does in the next verse. While the court sits, a disciple is out in the courtyard watching, and his own trial is about to begin.$n$),
(4143,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'luke',22,63,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 22:63 — *And the men that held Yahusha (Jesus) mocked him, and smote him.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Luke has the men who held him mocking and striking him, and sets it before the elders and chief priests gather at day. Matthew sets the spitting after the verdict. They agree that he was struck and made the target of a joke by the men who held him.$n$),
(4144,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'john',18,13,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + Hananyah's-questioning.$n$,$n$John 18:13 — *And led him away to Annas first; for he was father in law to Caiaphas, which was the high priest that same year.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Matthew goes straight to Caiaphas, and John says they led him *to Annas first*, the father in law of Caiaphas. John adds that Caiaphas was the one who counseled *it was expedient that one man should die for the people* (John 18:14), so what the court would decide had been said before he stood in it.$n$),
(4145,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'john',18,19,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + Hananyah's-questioning.$n$,$n$John 18:19 — *The high priest then asked Yahusha (Jesus) of his disciples, and of his doctrine.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) John gives the question the high priest put to him, *of his disciples, and of his doctrine*, and the answer, *I spake openly to the world* (John 18:20). He then sends the high priest to those who heard him, *ask them which heard me* (John 18:21), which is where witnesses belong.$n$),
(4146,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'psalms',110,1,$n$The substantial-right-hand-substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 110:1 — *Yahuah (LORD) said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) He is led to Caiaphas, and the answer he will give there is David's verse, *sitting on the right hand of power* (Matthew 26:64). David calls him *my Lord*, and Yahuah (the LORD) tells him to sit at his right hand, so two are in the verse, the one who speaks and the one who sits. The man led away bound is claiming that seat.$n$),
(4147,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'daniel',7,13,$n$The substantial-Son-of-Adam-coming-with-the-clouds substance per Red Line #12.$n$,$n$Daniel 7:13 — *I saw in the night visions, and, behold, one like the Son of Adam came with the clouds of heaven, and came to the Ancient of days, and they brought him near before him.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Led before Caiaphas, he answers with Daniel's vision: one *like the Son of Adam* who came *with the clouds of heaven* to the Ancient of days. Daniel's word is *like*, for the one who comes took on flesh and looked like a son of Adam, and the next verse says what is given him, *dominion, and glory, and a kingdom* (Daniel 7:14). The man standing bound before the high priest is saying that figure is himself.$n$),
(4148,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$*He openeth not his mouth.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Matthew says *Yahusha (Jesus) held his peace* (Matthew 26:63), and Isaiah had already written the man who is afflicted and yet opens not his mouth. Brought as a lamb to the slaughter, he is silent before the high priest because that is the servant's way.$n$),
(4149,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'isaiah',50,6,$n$*I gave my back to the smiters.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 50:6 — *I gave my back to the smiters, and my cheeks to them that plucked off the hair: I hid not my face from shame and spitting.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Matthew says *Then did they spit in his face, and buffeted him* (Matthew 26:67). Isaiah's speaker had said he hid not his face *from shame and spitting*, giving his back to the smiters. He gave himself to it, and nobody took the choice from him.$n$),
(4150,26,57,$n$And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.$n$,'deuteronomy',19,15,$n$The substantial-Tanakh-procedural-substance-of-witnesses.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 19:15 — *One witness shall not rise up against a man for any iniquity, or for any sin, in any sin that he sinneth: at the mouth of two witnesses, or at the mouth of three witnesses, shall the matter be established.* *And they that had laid hold on Yahusha (Jesus) led him away to Caiaphas the high priest, where the scribes and the elders were assembled.* (Matthew 26:57) Moses' rule is that a matter is established *at the mouth of two witnesses*. Matthew's council could not meet it, *though many false witnesses came, yet found they none* (Matthew 26:60), and the high priest asks *what further need have we of witnesses?* (Matthew 26:65). The Torah stood and their court did not keep it.$n$),
(4151,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'mark',14,66,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 14:66 — *And as Peter was beneath in the palace, there cometh one of the maids of the high priest:* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Mark names the woman as one of *the maids of the high priest* and has Peter *beneath in the palace*, in the courtyard below the room where Yahusha (Jesus) is being tried. Peter's first question comes from a servant of the house whose master is judging his teacher.$n$),
(4152,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'luke',22,54,$n$The Lukan-parallel + the King's-substantial-look at-Kefa.$n$,$n$Luke 22:54 — *Then took they him, and led him, and brought him into the high priest’s house. And Peter followed afar off.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Luke has Peter following *afar off* and adds what Matthew leaves out. After the denial Yahusha (Jesus) *turned, and looked upon Peter* (Luke 22:61), and Peter *went out, and wept bitterly* (Luke 22:62). The look is the way back.$n$),
(4153,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'john',18,15,$n$The Yochanan-narrative.$n$,$n$John 18:15 — *And Simon Peter followed Yahusha (Jesus), and so did another disciple: that disciple was known unto the high priest, and went in with Yahusha (Jesus) into the palace of the high priest.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) John says Peter did not go in alone. Another disciple, *known unto the high priest*, went in with Yahusha (Jesus) and then *spake unto her that kept the door, and brought in Peter* (John 18:16), so the door that opened to the question at the fire was opened for him by a friend.$n$),
(4154,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'john',18,25,$n$The Yochanan-narrative.$n$,$n$John 18:25 — *And Simon Peter stood and warmed himself. They said therefore unto him, Art not thou also one of his disciples? He denied it, and said, I am not.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) John puts Peter at the fire of coals among the servants and officers, and the question there is the one Matthew has, *Art not thou also one of his disciples?* The answer in John is a flat *I am not*.$n$),
(4155,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'matthew',26,33,$n$The substantial-prediction-of-the-denial.$n$,$n$Matthew 26:33 — *Peter answered and said unto him, Though all men shall be offended because of thee, yet will I never be offended.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Peter spoke at the supper with his whole heart, *yet will I never be offended*, and a few hours later he said *I do not know the man* (Matthew 26:72). Yahusha (Jesus) had told him exactly what was coming, *before the cock crow, thou shalt deny me thrice* (Matthew 26:34), so the fall was no surprise to him.$n$),
(4156,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'john',21,15,$n$The substantial-restoration-of-Kefa substance.$n$,$n$John 21:15 — *So when they had dined, Yahusha (Jesus) saith to Simon Peter, Simon, son of Jonah, lovest thou me more than these? He saith unto him, Yea, Adonai (Lord); thou knowest that I love thee. He saith unto him, Feed my lambs.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Peter denied three times and is asked three times, *lovest thou me?*, after the resurrection. Each answer gets a charge, *Feed my lambs*, and *Feed my sheep* (John 21:16). The sheep that stumbled is not cast off, he is given the sheep to tend.$n$),
(4157,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'psalms',51,17,$n$*A broken and a contrite heart.*$n$,$n$Psalm 51:17 — *The sacrifices of Elohim (God) are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart, O Elohim (God), thou wilt not despise.* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Peter went out and wept bitterly, and the psalm speaks to that. David wrote that *a broken and a contrite heart* is what Elohim (God) will not despise, and Peter, the sheep that stumbled, is met by that and not by condemnation.$n$),
(4158,26,69,$n$Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.$n$,'joel',2,12,$n$*Rend your heart, and not your garments.*$n$,$n$Joel 2:12 — *Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:* *Now Peter sat without in the palace: and a damsel came unto him, saying, You also were with Yahusha (Jesus) of Galilee.* (Matthew 26:69) Joel's call is *turn ye even to me with all your heart*, and a verse on, *rend your heart, and not your garments* (Joel 2:13). The high priest rent his clothes earlier in the chapter; the one who answers Joel's call is Peter, who went out and wept.$n$),
(4159,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'zechariah',11,12,$n$The substantial-Tanakh-anchor of-the-thirty-pieces-of-silver.$n$,$n$Zechariah 11:12 — *And I said unto them, If ye think good, give me my price; and if not, forbear. So they weighed for my price thirty pieces of silver.* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) The council that met at daybreak to put Yahusha (Jesus) to death is the same company that ends up holding thirty pieces of silver. In Zechariah the shepherd asks for his wages and is weighed exactly that sum, and the next line tells him where to throw it: *Cast it unto the potter* (Zechariah 11:13). It is the price put on the shepherd of the flock.$n$),
(4160,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'jeremiah',19,1,$n$The substantial-potter's-field substance.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 19:1 — *Thus saith Yahuah (LORD), Go and get a potter’s earthen bottle, and take of the ancients of the people, and of the ancients of the priests;* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) The same two groups meet in both places, the elders of the people and the elders of the priests. Yahuah (the LORD) sends Jeremiah to them with a potter's earthen bottle and a charge, that they had *filled this place with the blood of innocents* (Jeremiah 19:4). Matthew names Jeremiah over the potter's field, and this is the chapter where the potter and the blood stand together.$n$),
(4161,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'jeremiah',32,6,$n$The buying-of-the-field substance.$n$,$n$Jeremiah 32:6 — *And Jeremiah said, The word of Yahuah (LORD) came unto me, saying,* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) Matthew names Jeremiah over the field the priests bought, and this is the chapter where Jeremiah buys a field. He does it at the word of Yahuah (the LORD), when his uncle's son offers it, and in his own words he *weighed him the money, even seventeen shekels of silver* (Jeremiah 32:9). A field bought with weighed silver is the thread that runs between the two stories.$n$),
(4162,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'psalms',109,8,$n$*Let his days be few; and let another take his office* — Acts 1:20 substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 109:8 — *Let his days be few; and let another take his office.* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) This is David's verse about the man set against him, and Peter later takes it for Judas, who sat among the twelve. The council that morning needed one of the twelve to hand Yahusha (Jesus) over, and the verse says what happens to the office he leaves: another takes it.$n$),
(4163,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'acts',1,16,$n$The substantial-Yahudah-substance + Tehillim 69:25, 109:8.$n$,$n$Acts 1:16 — *Men and brethren, this scripture must needs have been fulfilled, which the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) by the mouth of David spake before concerning Judas, which was guide to them that took Yahusha (Jesus).* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) Peter says the scripture had to be fulfilled, spoken by the Ruach HaKodesh (Holy Spirit) through David *concerning Judas, which was guide to them that took Yahusha (Jesus)*. The chief priests and elders taking counsel in Matthew are the ones he guided them to. Judas was not a stranger to the work; he stood among the twelve.$n$),
(4164,27,1,$n$When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:$n$,'deuteronomy',23,18,$n$The substantial-Tanakh-money-substance-prohibition.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 23:18 — *Thou shalt not bring the hire of a whore, or the price of a dog, into the house of Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) for any vow: for even both these are abomination unto Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God).* *When the morning was come, all the chief priests and elders of the people took counsel against Yahusha (Jesus) to put him to death:* (Matthew 27:1) Later that morning the silver comes back to those same priests, and they will not put it in the treasury because it is the price of blood. The commandment behind their words keeps such money out of the house of Yahuah (the LORD). The Torah is right in this; it is the court that is wrong, for it kept the line carefully while the man the money paid for was being handed over.$n$),
(4165,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'mark',15,1,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 15:1 — *And straightway in the morning the chief priests held a consultation with the elders and scribes and the whole council, and bound Yahusha (Jesus), and carried him away, and delivered him to Pilate.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) Mark says who stood behind the hand-over: the chief priests with the elders and scribes and *the whole council* bound Yahusha (Jesus) and carried him to Pilate. When the governor asks if he is the King of the Yahudim (Jews), the question has been brought to him by the court and not made up in the room.$n$),
(4166,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'luke',23,1,$n$The Lukan-parallel + the substantial-Hordos (Herod)-trial.$n$,$n$Luke 23:1 — *And the whole multitude of them arose, and led him unto Pilate.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) Luke has them all rise and lead him to Pilate, and then he names the charge: *forbidding to give tribute to Cæsar, saying that he himself is Messiah (Christ) a King* (Luke 23:2). That is why Pilate asks the question he does in Matthew, and Yahusha's (Jesus's) answer is the same, *Thou sayest*. Luke goes on to a hearing before Herod that Matthew leaves out.$n$),
(4167,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'john',18,28,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + *what is truth?* + *my kingdom is not of this world*.$n$,$n$John 18:28 — *Then led they Yahusha (Jesus) from Caiaphas unto the hall of judgment: and it was early; and they themselves went not into the judgment hall, lest they should be defiled; but that they might eat the passover.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) John shows the rulers standing outside the governor's hall so as not to be defiled, because they meant to *eat the passover*. They had put an innocent man before a Roman and were careful about the cleanness of the feast. Nobody in the verse argues the Passover away; what is on trial is a court that kept the feast with one hand and handed Yahusha (Jesus) over with the other.$n$),
(4168,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$*He openeth not his mouth.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) Yahusha (Jesus) says *Thou sayest* to the question about a king, and when the chief priests and elders press their charges Matthew says *he answered nothing* (Matthew 27:12). Isaiah's servant is the same: afflicted, yet *he openeth not his mouth*, like a lamb brought to the slaughter. He answers what is true and lets the accusations go by.$n$),
(4169,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'deuteronomy',21,1,$n$The hand-washing-Tanakh-procedural.$n$,$n$Deuteronomy 21:1 — *If one be found slain in the land which Yahuah Elohayka (the LORD thy God) giveth thee to possess it, lying in the field, and it be not known who hath slain him:* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) Pilate washes his hands later in this same hearing, and he is borrowing a ceremony Moses gave. When a slain man is found and no one knows who killed him, the elders of the nearest city come forward and clear themselves. That ceremony is for blood whose killer is unknown. Matthew says Pilate knew it was envy that had delivered Yahusha (Jesus) up, so he took the gesture without the case it was made for.$n$),
(4170,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'acts',3,13,$n$*Pilate, when he was determined to let him go ... ye denied the Holy One and the Just.*$n$,$n$Acts 3:13 — *The Elohim (God) of Abraham, and of Isaac, and of Jacob, the Elohim (God) of our fathers, hath glorified his Son Yahusha (Jesus); whom ye delivered up, and denied him in the presence of Pilate, when he was determined to let him go.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) Peter's account agrees with Matthew's: Pilate *was determined to let him go*, and the delivering up and the denial came from those who pressed him. Peter calls Yahusha (Jesus) the Son whom the Elohim (God) of Abraham, of Isaac and of Jacob has glorified, so the one standing before the governor already belonged to the fathers.$n$),
(4171,27,11,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.$n$,'acts',4,25,$n$The substantial-gathering-against-the-Father's-anointed substance.$n$,$n$Acts 4:25 — *Who by the mouth of thy servant David hast said, Why did the heathen rage, and the people imagine vain things?* *And Yahusha (Jesus) stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Are you the King of the Yahudim (Jews)? And Yahusha (Jesus) said unto him, You say.* (Matthew 27:11) The believers in Acts pray David's psalm over this very trial. They name the kings and rulers gathered *against Yahuah (Lord), and against his Messiah (Christ)* (Acts 4:26), and then Herod, Pilate, the nations and the people together. In Matthew the chief priests, the elders and the governor are those rulers, and the one in front of the governor is the anointed.$n$),
(4172,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'mark',15,16,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 15:16 — *And the soldiers led him away into the hall, called Prætorium; and they call together the whole band.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) Mark names the hall, the Prætorium, where Matthew says only the common hall, and his *whole band* is the same company of soldiers gathered to mock one man. He then lists the purple, the crown of thorns and the salute, so the two accounts tell one scene, each with the blows set down plainly.$n$),
(4173,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'luke',23,26,$n$The Lukan-parallel + the substantial-women-of-Yerushalayim-substance.$n$,$n$Luke 23:26 — *And as they led him away, they laid hold upon one Simon, a Cyrenian, coming out of the country, and on him they laid the cross, that he might bear it after Yahusha (Jesus).* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) Luke names the man the soldiers seized on the road: Simon, a Cyrenian, coming in from the country, who was made to carry the cross *after Yahusha (Jesus)*. Matthew's soldiers have just finished their sport in the hall, and Luke shows what the way out looked like, with a stranger pressed into service and a great company, women among them, following behind.$n$),
(4174,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'john',19,17,$n$The Yochanan-narrative.$n$,$n$John 19:17 — *And he bearing his cross went forth into a place called the place of a skull, which is called in the Hebrew Golgotha:* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) John says he went out *bearing his cross* to the place of a skull, called in Hebrew Golgotha, where Matthew's soldiers take him once the robe is off. John tells that he began by bearing it himself and Matthew that Simon was found along the way, and both end the road at Golgotha.$n$),
(4175,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'isaiah',50,6,$n$*I gave my back to the smiters.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 50:6 — *I gave my back to the smiters, and my cheeks to them that plucked off the hair: I hid not my face from shame and spitting.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) In the hall the soldiers strike him and spit on him. Isaiah's servant speaks of just that: *I gave my back to the smiters*, he says, and *hid not my face from shame and spitting*. His word is *gave*, and it matches one who is not being dragged but is giving, the way Yahusha (Jesus) went through that hall.$n$),
(4176,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'isaiah',53,3,$n$The Suffering-Servant substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:3 — *He is despised and rejected of men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief: and we hid as it were our faces from him; he was despised, and we esteemed him not.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) The soldiers bow before a man they take for nobody. Isaiah says of the servant that he is *despised and rejected of men*, and that men *hid as it were our faces from him*. He is also *a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief*, and the hall shows how little the men in it thought of him.$n$),
(4177,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'isaiah',53,7,$n$The Suffering-Servant substance.$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:7 — *He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth: he is brought as a lamb to the slaughter, and as a sheep before her shearers is dumb, so he openeth not his mouth.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) Through the robe, the reed and the blows, Matthew gives Yahusha (Jesus) no word to say. Isaiah has the same silence: oppressed and afflicted, *yet he opened not his mouth*. The hall is the second place after Pilate's where that silence is tested, and it holds.$n$),
(4178,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'psalms',22,7,$n$The casting-of-lots + mocking substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 22:7 — *All they that see me laugh me to scorn: they shoot out the lip, they shake the head, saying,* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) David's psalm has the watchers laughing, shooting out the lip and shaking the head, and then it gives them words to say. All of it is around the cross: the soldiers laugh in the hall, the passers-by wag their heads, and the rulers say what the psalm already had in their mouths. David wrote the scene long before, from the inside.$n$),
(4179,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'psalms',22,16,$n$The casting-of-lots + mocking substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 22:16 — *For dogs have compassed me: the assembly of the wicked have inclosed me: they pierced my hands and my feet.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) The whole band gathered around one man in the hall reads like David's picture: *dogs have compassed me*, and *the assembly of the wicked have inclosed me*. The line that follows is *they pierced my hands and my feet*. The hall is the ring, and the road out of it ends at the nails.$n$),
(4180,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'genesis',3,15,$n$The thorns + Cross-bruising substance.$n$,$n$Genesis 3:15 — *And I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel.* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) The first promise in Genesis sets the serpent's seed against the woman's seed: *it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel*. Matthew does not quote it; read line on line with the hall, the enmity is out in the open there, and the verse says whose head is bruised in the end.$n$),
(4181,27,27,$n$Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.$n$,'genesis',3,18,$n$The thorns + Cross-bruising substance.$n$,$n$Genesis 3:18 — *Thorns also and thistles shall it bring forth to thee; and thou shalt eat the herb of the field;* *Then the soldiers of the governor took Yahusha (Jesus) into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.* (Matthew 27:27) Genesis names thorns and thistles as what the ground under the curse would bring forth. The soldiers plat thorns into a crown and set it on his head in that hall. Matthew does not point back to Genesis, so we read the two line on line: the thorns of the curse on the ground now lie on his head.$n$),
(4182,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'mark',15,29,$n$The Markan-parallel + *Eloi, Eloi*.$n$,$n$Mark 15:29 — *And they that passed by railed on him, wagging their heads, and saying, Ah, thou that destroyest the temple, and buildest it in three days,* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) The passers-by in both gospels wag their heads and throw the temple saying at him. Mark has one more sound, *Ah*, a cry of contempt, and the charge shouted from the road is the one the court could not make stand when it went looking for two agreeing witnesses. It has passed from the court to the crowd on the road.$n$),
(4183,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'luke',23,35,$n$The Lukan-parallel + the substantial-thief-substance + *Into thine hand I commit my spirit*.$n$,$n$Luke 23:35 — *And the people stood beholding. And the rulers also with them derided him, saying, He saved others; let him save himself, if he be Messiah (Christ), the chosen of Elohim (God).* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) Luke says who joined in: *the rulers also with them derided him*, saying *He saved others; let him save himself*. In Matthew the same words come from the chief priests, scribes and elders, so the rulers' voice is the one behind the wagging heads. Luke has them say *the chosen of Elohim (God)*, using the name of the one they say he is not.$n$),
(4184,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'john',19,25,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + *It is finished*.$n$,$n$John 19:25 — *Now there stood by the cross of Yahusha (Jesus) his mother, and his mother’s sister, Mary the wife of Cleophas, and Mary Magdalene.* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) While the passers-by wag their heads and the rulers sneer, John says who stands near, his mother and her sister and two women named Mary. Matthew sees the women from a distance, *many women were there beholding afar off* (Matthew 27:55), and John sets some of them at the cross itself. The mockers laughed, and these stayed.$n$),
(4185,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'psalms',22,1,$n$The substantial-Tehillim 22-as-a-whole — suffering-into-vindication.$n$,$n$Psalm 22:1 — *My Elohim (God), my Elohim (God), why hast thou forsaken me? why art thou so far from helping me, and from the words of my roaring?* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) The cry Matthew records is the opening line of David's psalm, and the men wagging their heads below him are speaking the lines David wrote for his mockers. Yahusha (Jesus) opens a psalm that does not end at being forsaken; it goes on to *I will declare thy name unto my brethren* (Psalm 22:22), so the cry begins the psalm and does not close it.$n$),
(4186,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'psalms',69,21,$n$The vinegar-substance.$n$,$n$Psalm 69:21 — *They gave me also gall for my meat; and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink.* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) David says *in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink*, and Matthew shows it twice: the vinegar mingled with gall offered at Golgotha, which he tasted and would not drink, and the sponge of vinegar lifted on a reed after his cry. The men at the cross are handing him David's cup.$n$),
(4187,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'amos',8,9,$n$*I will cause the sun to go down at noon.*$n$,$n$Amos 8:9 — *And it shall come to pass in that day, saith Adonai Yahuah (the Lord GOD), that I will cause the sun to go down at noon, and I will darken the earth in the clear day:* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) Amos says *in that day* the sun will go down at noon and the earth will be darkened *in the clear day*. Matthew has darkness over all the land from the sixth hour, which is noon, to the ninth, while he hangs there. The darkness is not weather but the day Amos names, with Yahuah (the LORD) behind it.$n$),
(4188,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'john',10,17,$n$*I lay down my life ... no man taketh it from me.*$n$,$n$John 10:17 — *Therefore doth my Father love me, because I lay down my life, that I might take it again.* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) The people at the cross read helplessness into what they see. Yahusha (Jesus) had already said what it was: *I lay down my life*, and his Father loves him for it, *that I might take it again*. The one they tell to save himself is giving his life, and the giving is something his Father loves in him.$n$),
(4189,27,39,$n$And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,$n$,'hebrews',5,7,$n$*Offered up prayers and supplications with strong crying and tears.*$n$,$n$Hebrews 5:7 — *Who in the days of his flesh, when he had offered up prayers and supplications with strong crying and tears unto him that was able to save him from death, and was heard in that he feared;* *And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,* (Matthew 27:39) The loud cries in Matthew are what Hebrews describes: in the days of his flesh he sent up *prayers and supplications* with strong crying and tears to the one who could save him from death, and the writer says it was heard. The mockers tell him to save himself; the verse shows him asking the one who could.$n$),
(4190,27,51,$n$And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom; and the earth did quake, and the rocks rent;$n$,'john',19,31,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + Zekharyah 12:10 citation.$n$,$n$John 19:31 — *The Yahudim (Jews) therefore, because it was the preparation, that the bodies should not remain upon the cross on the sabbath day, (for that sabbath day was an high day,) besought Pilate that their legs might be broken, and that they might be taken away.* *And, behold, the veil of the temple was rent in twain from the top to the bottom; and the earth did quake, and the rocks rent;* (Matthew 27:51) John shows the same afternoon from outside the temple: it was *the preparation*, with a Sabbath coming that was *an high day*, and men hurried to have the bodies off the crosses before it. The veil is torn and the day of preparation is still being kept; nothing in either verse treats the Sabbath as finished.$n$),
(4191,27,57,$n$When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:$n$,'mark',15,42,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 15:42 — *And now when the even was come, because it was the preparation, that is, the day before the sabbath,* *When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:* (Matthew 27:57) Mark explains the hour Matthew calls only evening: it was *the preparation, that is, the day before the sabbath*. Joseph moves at once, with the Sabbath coming on, and the body is laid in the tomb before it begins. The day was kept even in grief.$n$),
(4192,27,57,$n$When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:$n$,'luke',23,50,$n$The Lukan-parallel — Yoseph-substance + the substantial-substance-of-the-substantial-not-consenting-to-the-counsel.$n$,$n$Luke 23:50 — *And, behold, there was a man named Joseph, a counsellor; and he was a good man, and a just:* *When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:* (Matthew 27:57) Luke gives Joseph's character: *a counsellor; and he was a good man, and a just*. He sat in the very body that condemned Yahusha (Jesus), and Luke says *The same had not consented to the counsel and deed of them* (Luke 23:51). A court is a system, and there was a just man inside it who would not go along.$n$),
(4193,27,57,$n$When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:$n$,'john',19,38,$n$The Yochanan-narrative + Nikodimos.$n$,$n$John 19:38 — *And after this Joseph of Arimathaea, being a disciple of Yahusha (Jesus), but secretly for fear of the Yahudim (Jews), besought Pilate that he might take away the body of Yahusha (Jesus): and Pilate gave him leave. He came therefore, and took the body of Yahusha (Jesus).* *When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:* (Matthew 27:57) John adds that Joseph was a disciple *secretly for fear of the Yahudim (Jews)*. He was a Yahudi (Jew) himself, so John means the men who held the power, the court that had just put Yahusha (Jesus) to death. The man who had kept quiet goes in to the governor and asks for the body, and Pilate gives it.$n$),
(4194,27,57,$n$When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:$n$,'isaiah',53,9,$n$*Made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death.*$n$,$n$Isaiah 53:9 — *And he made his grave with the wicked, and with the rich in his death; because he had done no violence, neither was any deceit in his mouth.* *When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:* (Matthew 27:57) Matthew names the man who takes the body: a rich man named Joseph. Isaiah says the servant's grave was *with the rich in his death*, and gives the reason in the same breath: *he had done no violence*, and no deceit was in his mouth. The rich man's tomb is the place the verse expected for one who was innocent though he died among the wicked.$n$),
(4195,27,57,$n$When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:$n$,'matthew',28,11,$n$The substantial-Roman-watch substance — the substantial-Resurrection-witness.$n$,$n$Matthew 28:11 — *Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.* *When the even was come, there came a rich man of Arimathæa, named Joseph, who also himself was Yahusha's (Jesus’) disciple:* (Matthew 27:57) This chapter ends with the chief priests sealing the stone and setting a watch so that no one could say he was risen. The next chapter has the watch walking back into the city to tell those same chief priests *all the things that were done*. The system that sealed the tomb is the first to hear what happened at it, from its own guards.$n$),
(4196,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'matthew',12,38,$n$The sign-of-Yonah-substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 12:38 — *Then certain of the scribes and of the Pharisees answered, saying, Master, we would see a sign from thee.* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) The scribes and Pharisees asked for a sign, and Yahusha (Jesus) told them they would be given only one, Jonah's: *so shall the Son of Adam be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth* (Matthew 12:40). The two Marys going to look at the sepulchre as the first day of the week begins to dawn are standing where that sign comes due.$n$),
(4197,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'matthew',26,32,$n$*After I am risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.*$n$,$n$Matthew 26:32 — *But after I am risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) On the Mount of Olives Yahusha (Jesus) had already told his disciples, who were about to be scattered, where he would meet them once he was raised. The angel's word to the women, *he goeth before you into Galilee* (Matthew 28:7), is that same promise being kept, so the empty tomb breaks nothing he had not said.$n$),
(4198,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'john',20,17,$n$*Go to my brethren* — the King-identification-substance.$n$,$n$John 20:17 — *Yahusha (Jesus) saith unto her, Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father: but go to my brethren, and say unto them, I ascend unto my Father, and your Father; and to my Elohim (God), and your Elohim (God).* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) Mary Magdalene is at the sepulchre in Matthew, and John shows what she is handed to do when he meets her: carry word to *my brethren*. The women in Matthew get the same errand, *go tell my brethren* (Matthew 28:10), so the first witnesses of the risen one are sent by his own mouth, and sent to his brethren.$n$),
(4199,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'matthew',25,40,$n$*My brethren* — the substantial-`my-brethren` thread (FRAMEWORK-MAJOR from S87 Matt 25).$n$,$n$Matthew 25:40 — *And the King shall answer and say unto them, Verily I say unto you, Inasmuch as ye have done it unto one of the least of these my brethren, ye have done it unto me.* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) The word he gives the women at the tomb is *go tell my brethren* (Matthew 28:10), and it is the word he used at the judging of the sheep and the goats, where what is done to *the least of these my brethren* is counted as done to him. Matthew has no second meaning in mind; these are the same brethren in both places.$n$),
(4200,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'hebrews',2,11,$n$*He is not ashamed to call them brethren.*$n$,$n$Hebrews 2:11 — *For both he that sanctifieth and they who are sanctified are all of one: for which cause he is not ashamed to call them brethren,* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) At the tomb he says *my brethren* (Matthew 28:10), and Hebrews says what stands behind the word: the one who makes holy and those who are made holy are *all of one*, and that is why *he is not ashamed to call them brethren*. The word he gives the women is no courtesy; it names a kinship he owns.$n$),
(4201,28,1,$n$In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.$n$,'leviticus',23,15,$n$The Wave-Sheaf-Offering substance.$n$,$n$Leviticus 23:15 — *And ye shall count unto you from the morrow after the sabbath, from the day that ye brought the sheaf of the wave offering; seven sabbaths shall be complete:* *In the end of the sabbath, as it began to dawn toward the first day of the week, came Mary Magdalene and the other Mary to see the sepulchre.* (Matthew 28:1) Leviticus starts the counting *from the morrow after the sabbath*, the day after the sheaf of the wave offering is brought, and Matthew sets the women at the sepulchre as one sabbath ends and the first day of the week dawns. That puts the empty tomb on a morning after a sabbath, the morning the firstfruits count begins.$n$),
(4202,28,11,$n$Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.$n$,'matthew',27,62,$n$The substantial-guarding-of-the-tomb.$n$,$n$Matthew 27:62 — *Now the next day, that followed the day of the preparation, the chief priests and Pharisees came together unto Pilate,* *Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.* (Matthew 28:11) The chief priests who went to Pilate afraid that the disciples would *come by night, and steal him away* (Matthew 27:64) are the same men the watch reports to when the stone is rolled back. Their own fear becomes the tale they pay the soldiers to tell, and it is the leaders' work, not the work of the people who heard it.$n$),
(4203,28,11,$n$Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.$n$,'acts',4,1,$n$The substantial-Sanhedrin-substance walking-the-substance-of-the-substantial-Resurrection-suppression.$n$,$n$Acts 4:1 — *And as they spake unto the people, the priests, and the captain of the temple, and the Sadducees, came upon them,* *Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.* (Matthew 28:11) The priestly party that paid the guard to say the body was stolen are, in Acts, coming upon the disciples *grieved that they taught the people* (Acts 4:2) about the resurrection. Matthew shows a story being bought and Acts shows the witnesses being seized, yet many of the people who heard still believed. It is the system that wanted the resurrection quiet, not the crowds listening.$n$),
(4204,28,11,$n$Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.$n$,'acts',5,17,$n$The substantial-substance-of-the-substantial-leadership-substance' substantial-anti-Resurrection-narrative-substance.$n$,$n$Acts 5:17 — *Then the high priest rose up, and all they that were with him, (which is the sect of the Sadducees,) and were filled with indignation,* *Now when they were going, behold, some of the watch came into the city, and shewed unto the chief priests all the things that were done.* (Matthew 28:11) The high priest and those with him, named here as the sect of the Sadducees, are the same circle that took counsel and gave large money in Matthew. In Acts they fill with indignation and shut the apostles in the common prison, and an angel opens the doors and sends them back to speak to the people *all the words of this life* (Acts 5:20).$n$),
(4205,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'matthew',26,32,$n$*I will go before you into Galilee.*$n$,$n$Matthew 26:32 — *But after I am risen again, I will go before you into Galilee.* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) On the Mount of Olives he told his disciples he would go before them into Galilee, and the angel and then he himself repeated it to the women. Now the eleven do what they were told and go to the mountain *where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them*. A promise made before his death stands unbroken after it.$n$),
(4206,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'matthew',17,1,$n$The substantial-transfiguration substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 17:1 — *And after six days Yahusha (Jesus) taketh Peter, James, and John his brother, and bringeth them up into an high mountain apart,* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) These are two mountains, and Matthew does not call them one. On the first he took three disciples up apart and *his face did shine as the sun* (Matthew 17:2); on the second he meets all eleven where he had appointed, and they worship him. Each time he chooses the place and who comes up, and each time the disciples see who he is.$n$),
(4207,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'matthew',5,1,$n$The Sermon-on-the-Mount mountain-substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:1 — *And seeing the multitudes, he went up into a mountain: and when he was set, his disciples came unto him:* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) This is another mountain, not the same one, and Matthew never joins them. On the first he sat down, his disciples came to him, and he taught them; on the second he tells the eleven to teach *all things whatsoever I have commanded you* (Matthew 28:20). The teaching begun on the first mountain is what the eleven are sent to carry on from the second.$n$),
(4208,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'luke',24,36,$n$The Lukan-substance-of-the-substantial-substance-of-the-substantial-substance + the substantial-substance-of-the-doubting-substance.$n$,$n$Luke 24:36 — *And as they thus spake, Yahusha (Jesus) himself stood in the midst of them, and saith unto them, Peace be unto you.* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) Matthew says only *but some doubted* (Matthew 28:17). Luke shows what such doubt looked like: they *supposed that they had seen a spirit* (Luke 24:37), and he answers by telling them to handle him and see. He does not wait until they are sure before he comes and stands among them.$n$),
(4209,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'john',20,24,$n$The substantial-T'oma (Thomas)-substance + the substantial-substance-of-the-doubting-and-believing-substance.$n$,$n$John 20:24 — *But Thomas, one of the twelve, called Didymus, was not with them when Yahusha (Jesus) came.* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) Matthew says some doubted and goes no further. John gives the doubt a name: Thomas, who was not with the others the first time and said *I will not believe* (John 20:25) until he saw the nail prints. Yahusha (Jesus) does not drop him from the number but comes again and offers him his hands and his side.$n$),
(4210,28,16,$n$Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.$n$,'acts',1,15,$n$The substantial-Matthias-substance — the substantial-twelve-restored-substance.$n$,$n$Acts 1:15 — *And in those days Peter stood up in the midst of the disciples, and said, (the number of names together were about an hundred and twenty,)* *Then the eleven disciples went away into Galilee, into a mountain where Yahusha (Jesus) had appointed them.* (Matthew 28:16) Matthew counts the eleven on the mountain. In Acts the same men are named in the upper room, and Peter stands up among about a hundred and twenty to speak of Judas, who had been numbered with them and had a part in the ministry. The place Judas left is filled there, so the twelve stand whole again before the witnessing begins.$n$),
(4211,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'mark',16,14,$n$The Markan-parallel.$n$,$n$Mark 16:14 — *Afterward he appeared unto the eleven as they sat at meat, and upbraided them with their unbelief and hardness of heart, because they believed not them which had seen him after he was risen.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) Mark says the eleven were upbraided for unbelief and *hardness of heart*, and Matthew says *some doubted* (Matthew 28:17). In both gospels the sending comes right after, *Go ye into all the world* (Mark 16:15), so the ones he sends are the ones who had just been slow to believe, and the power Matthew names stands behind it.$n$),
(4212,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'luke',24,44,$n$The Lukan-parallel.$n$,$n$Luke 24:44 — *And he said unto them, These are the words which I spake unto you, while I was yet with you, that all things must be fulfilled, which were written in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) Before Luke's sending he sets the law of Moses, the prophets and the psalms in front of the disciples as the words written concerning him, and then repentance is to be preached *among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem* (Luke 24:47). The authority Matthew records on the mountain goes out with the old scriptures behind it, not with a new teaching in their place.$n$),
(4213,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'john',20,21,$n$The Yochanan-parallel.$n$,$n$John 20:21 — *Then said Yahusha (Jesus) to them again, Peace be unto you: as my Father hath sent me, even so send I you.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) Matthew has the power given to him, and John has the errand he was sent on: *as my Father hath sent me*, so he sends the disciples. The order is the same in both, the Father first, then the Son, then the disciples; he speaks as one who has a Father, and the authority he hands on is one he received.$n$),
(4214,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'psalms',110,1,$n$The substantial-right-hand-of-power.$n$,$n$Psalm 110:1 — *Yahuah (LORD) said unto my Lord, Sit thou at my right hand, until I make thine enemies thy footstool.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) In David's psalm Yahuah (the LORD) speaks to one David calls *my Lord* and tells him to sit at his right hand. In Matthew the risen one says all power *is given* to him. In both there is a giver and one who receives, the Father unseen and the Son who is seen, and the psalm says it is the giver who makes the enemies a footstool.$n$),
(4215,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'psalms',8,6,$n$*Thou madest him to have dominion.*$n$,$n$Psalm 8:6 — *Thou madest him to have dominion over the works of thy hands; thou hast put all things under his feet:* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) The psalm asks about *the son of Adam, that thou visitest him* (Psalm 8:4) and says of him *thou hast put all things under his feet*. In Matthew the risen Son of Adam says all power in heaven and in earth has been given him. In both the dominion is put under him by the one who gives it; it is not taken.$n$),
(4216,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'matthew',1,21,$n$The substantial-Yahusha-saves + Immanuel substance bookended-at-Matt 28:20.$n$,$n$Matthew 1:21 — *And she shall bring forth a son, and thou shalt call his name Yahusha (JESUS): for he shall save his people from their sins.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) The angel gave the name before he was born: Yahusha (Jesus), Yahuah (the LORD) saves, *for he shall save his people from their sins*. The one who says *all power is given unto me* at the end of the book is that same Yahusha (Jesus), risen, and he closes with *I am with you alway* (Matthew 28:20).$n$),
(4217,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'matthew',1,23,$n$The substantial-Yahusha-saves + Immanuel substance bookended-at-Matt 28:20.$n$,$n$Matthew 1:23 — *Behold, a virgin shall be with child, and shall bring forth a son, and they shall call his name Emmanuel, which being interpreted is, Elohim (God) with us.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) At the start he is called *Elohim (God) with us*, and at the end he says *I am with you alway* (Matthew 28:20). The book opens and closes on the same promise. The one who says all power is given him is the one named as with them from his birth, and he receives that power from the Father, for he is Elohim (God) and has a Father.$n$),
(4218,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'matthew',5,17,$n$The substantial-anti-antinomian declaration directly-closed-by-Matt 28:20.$n$,$n$Matthew 5:17 — *Think not that I am come to destroy the law, or the prophets: I am not come to destroy, but to fulfil.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) At the start of his teaching he says he did not come *to destroy the law, or the prophets*, and adds that *one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law* (Matthew 5:18). The last thing he tells the eleven is to teach *all things whatsoever I have commanded you* (Matthew 28:20), so what they carry out is the law he said would not pass, not a replacement for it.$n$),
(4219,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'matthew',7,21,$n$The substantial-doing-the-will-of-the-Father substance.$n$,$n$Matthew 7:21 — *Not every one that saith unto me, Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord), shall enter into the kingdom of heaven; but he that doeth the will of my Father which is in heaven.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) Saying *Adonai (Lord), Adonai (Lord)* is not what opens the kingdom; doing *the will of my Father* is. The charge in Matthew's last verses, *all things whatsoever I have commanded you* (Matthew 28:20), is that same doing handed on to be taught, so the eleven are sent to teach obedience and not a name spoken without it.$n$),
(4220,28,18,$n$And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.$n$,'john',14,15,$n$The substantial-Comforter + the substantial-keeping-of-the-commandments-substance.$n$,$n$John 14:15 — *If ye love me, keep my commandments.* *And Yahusha (Jesus) came and spake unto them, saying, All power is given unto me in heaven and in earth.* (Matthew 28:18) John has him say that love for him shows in keeping his commandments, and Matthew ends with him telling the eleven to teach others to observe *all things whatsoever I have commanded you* (Matthew 28:20). The charge is not something added after the cross; it is the same keeping he had called love before it.$n$);
INSERT INTO _s496_b5_t VALUES
('matthew-26-the-plot-and-the-passover-lamb',$n$The plot against Yahusha (Jesus) and the passover lamb$n$,$n$It ain't new. Two days before the feast, Yahusha (Jesus) says to his disciples:
